---
import "../styles/globals.css";
---

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="/favicon.svg" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <title>Nima Soufiani — Full-Stack Engineer</title>
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="Full-stack engineer in London. I design and ship products end to end — iOS, web, and the infrastructure underneath."
    />
  </head>
  <body class="bg-[var(--color-dark)] text-[var(--color-accent)]">
    <main class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6">

      <!-- Hero -->
      <section class="min-h-screen flex flex-col justify-center py-24">
        <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-6xl font-bold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-6">
          Nima Soufiani
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl md:text-2xl text-[var(--color-secondary)] mb-8 max-w-xl">
          Full-stack engineer in London. I design and ship products end to
          end — iOS, web, and the infrastructure underneath.
        </p>
        <p class="text-lg text-[var(--color-accent)]/80 mb-10 max-w-xl leading-relaxed">
          Two products in production: a compliance-heavy professional network
          for corporate boards, and an iOS app live on the App Store.
          Previously built in green energy and financial services.
        </p>
        <p class="text-lg font-semibold text-[var(--color-highlight)] mb-10">
          Available for contract and permanent roles.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-x-6 gap-y-2 text-[var(--color-secondary)]">
          <a href="mailto:nimasoufiani@gmail.com" class="hover:text-[var(--color-highlight)] transition-colors underline underline-offset-4">
            nimasoufiani@gmail.com
          </a>
          <a href="https://github.com/Nimzyow" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="hover:text-[var(--color-highlight)] transition-colors underline underline-offset-4">
            GitHub
          </a>
          <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/nima-soufiani-555a76198/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="hover:text-[var(--color-highlight)] transition-colors underline underline-offset-4">
            LinkedIn
          </a>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Case study — Olympo -->
      <section class="py-20 border-t border-[var(--color-secondary)]/20">
        <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-2">
          Olympo
        </h2>
        <p class="text-lg text-[var(--color-highlight)] mb-1">
          A professional network built for corporate boards, not general careers.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm text-[var(--color-secondary)] mb-8">
          Co-founded with Eleonora Labita. In production, pre-launch.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-sm uppercase tracking-wide text-[var(--color-secondary)] mb-2">The problem</h3>
        <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[var(--color-accent)]/90 mb-8">
          Companies need qualified, trustworthy board members. Board-ready
          professionals need a structured way to find the right seats. But
          board appointments aren't job applications — they involve sensitive
          credentials, regulatory obligations, and a level of trust that a
          listings site can't carry. The product had to be built around
          verification and governance from the first commit, not bolted on
          later.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-sm uppercase tracking-wide text-[var(--color-secondary)] mb-2">What that meant in practice</h3>
        <ul class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[var(--color-accent)]/90 mb-8 space-y-4 list-disc list-outside pl-5">
          <li>
            <span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)]">Verification</span> runs through a
            custom admin interface rather than an off-the-shelf KYC vendor —
            documents move through a <span class="italic">pending → verified</span>
            state in the database, and nothing is treated as trusted until an
            admin has reviewed it.
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)]">Data model</span> runs on DynamoDB
            behind a fully serverless stack (SST on AWS), so cost tracks
            actual usage instead of idle infrastructure — a deliberate choice
            for a pre-launch product with no users yet.
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)]">Sensitive data</span> — CVs,
            identity documents, board credentials — lives in a private S3
            bucket with no public access; uploads and admin downloads go
            through short-lived presigned URLs, and everything is kept in the
            appropriate EU region to match the compliance rules the product
            is built around.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h3 class="text-sm uppercase tracking-wide text-[var(--color-secondary)] mb-2">Stack</h3>
        <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[var(--color-accent)]/90 mb-8">
          SST (Serverless Stack), Next.js, AWS — S3, DynamoDB, Cognito.
        </p>

        <!-- TODO(nima): what's actually next, and one honest "what I'd do differently" line. -->

        <h3 class="text-sm uppercase tracking-wide text-[var(--color-secondary)] mb-2">Where it stands</h3>
        <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[var(--color-accent)]/90">
          Deployed to production and feature-complete for launch — no users
          yet.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Case study — Recommends -->
      <section class="py-20 border-t border-[var(--color-secondary)]/20">
        <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-2">
          Recommends
        </h2>
        <p class="text-lg text-[var(--color-highlight)] mb-1">
          Find the dish, not just the restaurant.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm text-[var(--color-secondary)] mb-8">
          My own product. Live on the iOS App Store.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-sm uppercase tracking-wide text-[var(--color-secondary)] mb-2">The idea</h3>
        <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[var(--color-accent)]/90 mb-8">
          Restaurant reviews rate the venue. Nobody rates the plate.
          Recommends is photo-backed, dish-level recommendations from people
          who actually ate the thing — so you arrive knowing what to order,
          not just where to go.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-sm uppercase tracking-wide text-[var(--color-secondary)] mb-2">What I built</h3>
        <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[var(--color-accent)]/90 mb-4">
          A native iOS app backed by a Python service — Celery for async
          ingest, Redis for scheduling, SpatiaLite for geo queries — with
          Gemini wired in for dish extraction. Two problems drove the
          architecture:
        </p>
        <ul class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[var(--color-accent)]/90 mb-8 space-y-4 list-disc list-outside pl-5">
          <li>
            <span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)]">Canonical dish identity.</span>
            Menus and posts use messy, inconsistent names, so the system
            turns photos into per-restaurant dish SKUs: Gemini suggests
            dishes on upload, Celery later extracts a full inventory, then
            exact and fuzzy matching (≥0.85 similarity) merges duplicates so
            votes and photos attach to the dish, not the post — entity
            resolution plus multimodal AI, so discovery ranks real dishes
            instead of one-off captions.
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)]">Local discovery under SpatiaLite constraints.</span>
            Nearby ranking combines distance and social signal on SpatiaLite,
            not PostGIS, so dietary filters can't safely ride inside the same
            annotated geo query and are matched outside the ORM instead.
            Location for digests is privacy-throttled by time and distance
            gates rather than raw GPS on every ping.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h3 class="text-sm uppercase tracking-wide text-[var(--color-secondary)] mb-2">Where it stands</h3>
        <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[var(--color-accent)]/90 mb-8">
          Shipped through App Store review and live, with real
          recommendations across London.
        </p>

        <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-4">
          <a href="https://apps.apple.com/gb/app/recommends/id6740585961" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-[var(--color-secondary)] hover:text-[var(--color-highlight)] transition-colors underline underline-offset-4">
            App Store →
          </a>
          <a href="https://recommends.app" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-[var(--color-secondary)] hover:text-[var(--color-highlight)] transition-colors underline underline-offset-4">
            recommends.app →
          </a>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- How I work with AI -->
      <section class="py-20 border-t border-[var(--color-secondary)]/20">
        <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-8">
          How I work with AI
        </h2>
        <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[var(--color-accent)]/90 mb-4">
          I build with AI tooling heavily, which mostly means I move faster on
          the parts that were never the interesting bit. On Recommends,
          instead of building OCR, NER, and custom matching by hand, I wired
          Gemini into a hybrid pipeline — fast menu suggestions on upload,
          Celery for a full inventory pass later, then exact-name normalizing
          followed by Gemini fuzzy merge (≥0.85) to create the dish SKU. That
          path took days of integration and orchestration, not weeks of model
          work.
        </p>
        <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[var(--color-accent)]/90">
          The tradeoff: the fuzzy match fails open — if the model errors or
          is unsure, it creates a new dish rather than risk a bad merge. That
          protects the vote graph from false collisions, but it means
          duplicate SKUs still turn up when names are ambiguous ("pad thai"
          vs. "pad thai chicken"). Deterministic matching owns the happy
          path; AI only bridges the messy middle.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- About -->
      <section class="py-20 border-t border-[var(--color-secondary)]/20">
        <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-8">
          About
        </h2>
        <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[var(--color-accent)]/90 mb-6">
          I'm a full-stack engineer based in London. I've worked in green
          energy and financial services — regulated, detail-heavy
          environments where getting it wrong is expensive — and I now build
          products end to end, from data model to App Store listing.
        </p>
        <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[var(--color-accent)]/90 mb-6">
          I speak English, Japanese, and Persian, and I'm learning Italian.
          If you're working across those markets, that tends to matter more
          than it looks like it should.
        </p>
        <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[var(--color-accent)]/90 mb-6">
          "Forever in transit" is about a love of travel — I'm always
          somewhere between here and there. Outside of client work I build
          all sorts of things for fun, lately leaning into game development;
          I mostly just like the process of building something from scratch
          and watching people react to it.
        </p>
        <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[var(--color-accent)]">
          <span class="font-semibold">What I'm looking for:</span> contract or
          permanent work with startups and small product teams, based in
          London, remote, or hybrid — with a soft spot for stints in Europe
          (Nice, Milan, Barcelona) and Tokyo.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Contact -->
      <section class="py-20 border-t border-[var(--color-secondary)]/20">
        <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-8">
          Contact
        </h2>
        <p class="text-lg mb-4">
          <a href="mailto:nimasoufiani@gmail.com" class="text-[var(--color-highlight)] hover:underline underline-offset-4">
            nimasoufiani@gmail.com
          </a>
        </p>
        <p class="text-base text-[var(--color-secondary)] flex gap-4">
          <a href="https://github.com/Nimzyow" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="hover:text-[var(--color-highlight)] transition-colors underline underline-offset-4">
            GitHub
          </a>
          <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/nima-soufiani-555a76198/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="hover:text-[var(--color-highlight)] transition-colors underline underline-offset-4">
            LinkedIn
          </a>
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Footer — live performance flourish -->
      <footer class="py-12 border-t border-[var(--color-secondary)]/20 text-center">
        <p class="text-sm text-[var(--color-secondary)]/70">
          © {new Date().getFullYear()} Nima Soufiani ·
          <span id="perf-flourish"> </span>
        </p>
      </footer>
    </main>

    <script>
      // One quiet, real flourish: this page's own load time, measured live.
      window.addEventListener("load", () => {
        const nav = performance.getEntriesByType("navigation")[0] as PerformanceNavigationTiming | undefined;
        const el = document.getElementById("perf-flourish");
        if (nav && el) {
          const ms = Math.round(nav.loadEventEnd - nav.startTime);
          el.textContent = `this page loaded in ${ms}ms`;
        }
      });
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
